    "id": 2000023,
    "title": "What Gen Z Actually Wants From a Church and why Its Not what you Think",
    "slug": "27-what-gen-z-actually-wants-from-a-church-and-why-its-not-what-you-think",
    "body": "They are showing up.\n\nAfter a decade of obituaries for young adult faith, the surveys keep finding Gen Z in the pews — at rates that meet or exceed the Millennials just ahead of them. Why they are returning is its own story. This piece is about the harder thing: what happens after they walk in. A generation can arrive and be lost again in eighteen months, and whether they stay has almost nothing to do with the welcome video.\n\nThe question is not how to attract them. Many of them are already in the parking lot. The question is whether the church they enter can receive what they bring — the questions, the wounds from previous church experience, and a trained eye for the gap between what is performed and what is real. They grew up inside the curated feed. They know staging when they see it. The church that wants to keep them will have to change things that are more expensive than the music.\n\n## Worship That Tells the Truth\n\nStart with the service itself, because that is where they take the first measurement.\n\nThe most consistent finding about this generation and institutions is that they do not expect perfection. They expect honesty — including honesty about failure, uncertainty, and the parts of faith that do not resolve on schedule. A church receives them by worshiping honestly: by letting the full texture of human experience into the room instead of sanding it off in rehearsal.\n\nScripture is already there, waiting. Psalm 88 moves through fourteen verses of sustained darkness and ends without a turn toward praise. Job's friends, with their tidy explanations, are the ones the book indicts. Matthew records that when the risen Jesus met the eleven on the mountain, \"they worshiped him, but some doubted\" (Matthew 28:17) — worship and doubt in the same verse, the same room, the same people. The canon contains more lament and confusion than most Sunday services permit. Restoring it is not a concession to a demographic. It is a recovery of what we cut.\n\n## Doubt With a Spine\n\nThe anxious counterargument deserves its hearing: if the church becomes a community defined by uncertainty, it has stopped being the church. Peter did not stand up at Pentecost and hedge. A congregation that will never make the specific claims of Christianity — Jesus is Lord, the resurrection happened, the gospel is true — for fear of discomforting its young adults is not being hospitable. It is being cowardly.\n\nThe concern is right, and it does not require choosing. What this generation trusts is not certainty and not uncertainty. It is integration — the leader who can say \"I believe this with my whole life\" and also \"I have had nights when I could not feel the truth of it and kept praying anyway.\" Conviction, with the questions allowed in the room. That is not a compromise between faith and doubt. It is what mature faith has always looked like up close.\n\n## Build the Table Before the Stage\n\nGen Z has access to more preaching, more worship music, and more theological content than any generation in the history of the church. No one drives forty-five minutes for a sermon they could hear in bed. What only physical presence provides is being known — belonging to a people, having a seat that is noticed when it is empty.\n\nSo the receiving church spends on community infrastructure before production quality. The small group that actually functions. The dinner table. The friendship that exists on Tuesday without a program requiring it. Jonathan Haidt's account of the great rewiring of childhood in *The Anxious Generation* (2024) names what pastors now see weekly: this is, by its own report, the loneliest generation in memory. The church that offers genuine, demanding community is not providing something nice. It is providing something they are starving for and cannot find at any price elsewhere.\n\n## Trust Them With Real Things\n\nThey are impatient with the consumer model — come, receive, leave — in a way their parents were not. They want to contribute. The churches that keep them find ways to hand over genuine ministry: not token youth committees, not watered-down volunteer slots, but real responsibility with real stakes.\n\nThat means tolerating the messiness of inexperience. It means leadership pathways that move a young adult from attending to contributing to leading on a timeframe that respects both their capacity and their unwillingness to wait a decade for a seat at the table. The church that trusts them with real things earns their real investment. The church that asks them only to attend has told them what it thinks they are.\n\n## What It Looks Like\n\nA woman in her mid-twenties started attending our church after two years of attending nowhere. She had grown up in a church she described as \"clean\" — theologically tidy, emotionally polished, efficient. \"I never saw anyone bleed there,\" she told me.\n\nShe stayed because of a small-group conversation in which a man ten years older said, out loud, that he had no idea how to pray in a season of depression and was doing it anyway. She had never heard a Christian say that before. It was, she said, the most credible thing she had ever heard in a church building.\n\nShe now leads that group.\n\n## The Data Point Is Not a Destiny\n\nThe rise in Gen Z attendance is an opening, not an outcome. The same generation that came will leave again if what it finds is a presentation of community rather than a community — if the honesty stops at the branding, if the doubt is managed rather than met, if the responsibility on offer is decorative.\n\nReceiving them well requires the church to be what it claims to be. That is not a communications challenge. It is a discipleship challenge, and it was ours before they arrived.\n\nGen Z is not looking for a better product. They are looking for a real community. The church that has built one — honestly, slowly, imperfectly, at cost — has something to offer. The church that has built a presentation of one does not.\n\nThe difference is visible from the parking lot. And Gen Z, more than any generation in recent memory, knows how to look.",
