    "id": 2000043,
    "title": "When Short Term Mission Trips Help and when They Hurt",
    "slug": "41-when-short-term-mission-trips-help-and-when-they-hurt",
    "body": "Somewhere on your church calendar there is probably a trip. The dates are set. The fundraising letters may already be drafted.\n\nThis is not the argument about whether short-term missions mostly serve the people they visit or the people they send. That argument matters, the honest version of it is uncomfortable, and every missions leader should sit with it. But a second, working question remains after the argument, and it is the one this piece takes up: the trip is happening. Whether it helps or hurts will be decided now, in the planning, months before anyone boards a plane.\n\n## The Three Conditions Under Which Trips Help\n\nThe trips that genuinely serve their hosts share a small set of features, and the features are structural, not sentimental.\n\nThe first is a long-term partnership. The team builds on an ongoing relationship between sending and receiving churches, contributes to work the receiving community has itself identified as a priority, and operates under the leadership of national believers rather than assuming its own. The trip is one week inside a ten-year relationship, not a transaction with photographs.\n\nThe second is genuinely scarce skill. A medical team providing care that is actually unavailable locally. Specialized training no one nearby can offer. The test is blunt: does this team supply what no local worker can, or does it displace a local worker who needed the wage? A crew of unskilled American teenagers building a wall that local masons could build better, faster, and for pay is not help. It is a cross-cultural experience purchased at the expense of the local economy.\n\nThe third condition is honesty about formation. Some trips exist primarily to form the participants — to put comfortable American Christians inside the global church and the poverty it works within, because that encounter permanently reshapes a person's understanding of the gospel. Such a trip can be legitimate. But only if it is named for what it is, the host community consents to it and is compensated for its time, and the visitors arrive as learners rather than rescuers.\n\n## How Trips Hurt\n\nThe harms are documented — Steve Corbett and Brian Fikkert in *When Helping Hurts* (2009), Robert Lupton in *Toxic Charity* (2011) — and they follow patterns any planning team can check itself against. Unskilled labor that displaces local wages. Predetermined programs implemented without reference to what the community asked for. Dependency: the community that learns to schedule its needs around the annual arrival of Americans, so that the yearly medical team becomes the standing argument against staffing the local clinic. And the subtlest harm — the trip as spiritual commodity, producing testimonies and social media for the senders while the hosts return to the structural poverty a week of enthusiasm has not altered.\n\nI have been on some version of these trips. I came home with the photographs and an enlarged sense of my own generosity. I did not ask, while I was there, whether my presence was the best use of the money it cost to send me. I should have asked.\n\n## Five Questions Before the Deposit Is Paid\n\nWhat has the receiving community asked for — in its own voice, not filtered through an agency brochure? If no one on the planning team can answer this, the planning has started at the wrong end.\n\nWhat would it cost to hire local people to do what the team plans to do? If the answer is \"a fraction of the airfare,\" have the honest conversation about who the trip is for — and consider sending the difference.\n\nWho holds authority on the ground? If the national leader directs the team's work, the trip serves the mission. If he merely hosts a plan built in an American church office, the trip serves the trip.\n\nWhat surrounds the week? A trip with no relationship before it and no engagement after it is almost certainly benefiting the participants more than the hosts. The strongest pattern practitioners recommend is simple: raise more than the trip costs, so that money remains in the community and continues the work after the team is gone.\n\nHow will it be evaluated? By the transformation of the participants, or by benefit the receiving community would itself name? If the honest answer is the first, say so out loud — and then design the trip so the hosts are served rather than used in the process.\n\n## Twelve Years In\n\nA pastor I know led a mid-sized suburban church with an active short-term program. He took teams to the same Central American community for twelve consecutive years. He knew the national pastor's name, his wife's name, their children's names. He had stayed in their home.\n\nHe told me that for the first four or five years his teams came with plans and left with photographs and produced, by his own honest accounting, very little the community could not have done better and cheaper without them. What changed everything was a conversation in year six, when the national pastor said — with the mixture of grace and directness that only real friendship permits — \"What we need from you is not your volunteers. We need your pastors' network. We need help getting our young leaders into theological training we cannot reach from here.\"\n\nThe program changed. The trips became smaller and were about connecting rather than building. The logistics money began funding scholarships for local seminary students. The church in Michigan became a partner instead of a periodic benefactor.\n\nThat is what a trip looks like when the receiving community is finally allowed to define it.\n\nThe communities on the receiving end of American mission trips deserve that level of care, and the gospel deserves to be pursued with enough wisdom to genuinely serve the people it claims to love. Start with what they asked for. End with whether they were better served than you were formed. Let the distance between those two questions be the work of this year's planning.",
    "excerpt": "Somewhere on your church calendar there is probably a trip. The dates are set. The fundraising letters may already be drafted.",
