    "id": 2000045,
    "title": "How National Pastors are Redefining what it Means to Be a Missionary",
    "slug": "43-how-national-pastors-are-redefining-what-it-means-to-be-a-missionary",
    "body": "The argument is over. The evidence ended it.\n\nThe center of global Christianity has moved — south into Africa and Latin America, east into Asia — and the fastest gospel advance on earth is now led by national pastors reaching their own peoples with a depth of language, trust, and cultural understanding no outsider can carry in. The church in Africa grew from under ten million Christians in 1900 to hundreds of millions today. The church in China grew for decades after every Western missionary was expelled in 1949 — grew most, in fact, in the years when no one from outside could reach it. William Carey asked the English church in 1792 to take the conversion of the nations seriously; the answer to his prayer turned out to be, in large part, men and women whose names the Western church has never learned.\n\nWhat remains is the harder and humbler question. If the national pastor is the primary agent of the mission, what is left for the Western church to do? Not in principle — in practice. In budget lines, in relationships, in the next missions-committee meeting.\n\n## Ask First, Then Believe the Answer\n\nWhen national pastors in underresourced regions are actually asked what they need — asked, not told — the answers are consistent, and they are not what our structures assume.\n\nThey do not ask for our church planters. They ask for theological training that engages their actual context — their religious neighbors, their political pressures, the questions their congregations carry — rather than a transplant of a Western seminary curriculum. They ask for peer community: other pastors who understand the specific weight of their work, the companionship a Western pastor takes for granted and a rural Ethiopian or north Indian pastor may lack entirely. They ask for funding structured to build capacity rather than dependence — scholarships and training and infrastructure, not salaries that vanish the year the Western church's attention moves on. And beneath all of it, sometimes in words and sometimes only in the fatigue under the words, they ask for the dignity of being treated as partners in the mission rather than beneficiaries of our generosity.\n\nNone of that is exotic. All of it is harder than sending a team.\n\n## Four Questions for the Missions Budget\n\nTake the missions budget — the actual document — and ask four questions of every line.\n\nWho set the direction? If the strategy was written in an American conference room and the national partner was recruited to execute it, the relationship is staffing, not partnership. The test is simple and uncomfortable: when the national pastor and the sending church disagree about priorities, whose judgment governs?\n\nWhat does this dollar build? A salary paid from abroad creates a dependence that ends when the giving ends — and it ends; it nearly always ends. A scholarship, a training program, a peer network, a translated library: these remain. The question is not whether the money is generous. It is whether the money leaves anything behind that does not require more money to survive.\n\nWhat happens when we leave? Roland Allen pressed this test on the church in 1912, in *Missionary Methods: St. Paul's or Ours?*, and it has not aged: Paul planted churches, entrusted them to the Spirit and to local leaders, and left. If the honest answer is that the work collapses when we withdraw, we have not built a church's capacity. We have built our own necessity.\n\nWould we accept this arrangement in reverse? If a wealthy foreign church funded our ministry on the condition that it approve our plans, receive our reports, and host our leaders for photographs, we would recognize the arrangement immediately. We would call it patronage. The national pastor recognizes it too. He is usually too gracious, and too much in need, to say so.\n\n## The Failure Modes\n\nThe failures here rarely look like failures. They look like warm relationships.\n\nThere is the salaried partner, who cannot say a true hard thing to the people who fund his family's food. There is the junior partner, present at the strategy meeting but not weighted in it, whose local knowledge is treated as color rather than as expertise. There is the showcase partner, flown in to Western conferences to stand as evidence of the church's global reach, spending his scarce time validating our generosity. And there is the trip-shaped partnership, which exists at full strength for one week a year and as a line item the other fifty-one.\n\nI have helped build at least one of these. The warmth of the relationship concealed the asymmetry of it, and the asymmetry was the point I could not see, because I was standing on the higher end.\n\n## What It Looks Like When It Works\n\nIt looks like training designed around the questions the national pastor actually faces, delivered in ways he can access without leaving the people he serves. It looks like peer networks that connect him to other pastors across his region and beyond it — the model the Pastors Connection Network is built on: theological training in context, companionship across borders, funding aimed at capacity, and direction set by the pastor who lives there. It looks like the Mekane Yesus church in Ethiopia, which counts its members in the millions and plants congregations in regions no Western agency has entered. Nothing about that work waits on our permission.\n\nThe partner's question is never \"what should they do?\" It is \"what do they need to keep doing it?\" — asked, answered, and believed.\n\nThe Great Commission does not name the senders' nationality. It names the scope — all nations — and the authority under which the sending happens (Matthew 28:18-20). The Spirit has been filling that scope without waiting for our organizational charts.\n\nWe were never the center of this work. We were servants of it. The budget is where a church admits that, or does not.",
