    "id": 2000086,
    "title": "Church Megaproject Widow Mite",
    "slug": "church-megaproject-widow-mite",
    "body": "Jesus sat down opposite the treasury and watched.\n\nThat detail in Mark 12 is worth pausing on. The treasury stood in the Court of Women — the area of the temple open to all Israel, not only the priests. The Mishnah (Shekalim 6:5) describes thirteen trumpet-shaped receptacles there, each labeled for its purpose: wood for the altar, frankincense, gold for the mercy seat. The coins rang as they fell. Jesus sat, and watched the crowd give: the rich putting in much, and a widow putting in two *lepta* — the smallest coins in circulation, together worth a fraction of a day's wage.\n\n\"Truly, I say to you, this poor widow has put in more than all those who are contributing to the offering box. For they all contributed out of their abundance, but she out of her poverty has put in everything she had, all she had to live on\" (Mark 12:43-44).\n\nThe standard sermon reaches sacrificial giving and stops. The text does not stop there. The preacher willing to read the frame around the widow will find the scene harder, and better, than the stewardship-series version — the frame Mark built, the interpretive debate the church still carries, and the prophetic tradition the whole scene stands in.\n\n## The Frame Mark Built\n\nImmediately before the widow, Jesus issues a warning: \"Beware of the scribes, who like to walk around in long robes and like greetings in the marketplaces and have the best seats in the synagogues and the places of honor at feasts, who devour widows' houses and for a pretense make long prayers. They will receive the greater condemnation\" (Mark 12:38-40).\n\nThey devour widows' houses. Then, in the next breath of the narrative, a widow appears and gives her last two coins to the temple those scribes represent.\n\nAnd immediately after: the disciples marvel at the building. \"Look, Teacher, what wonderful stones and what wonderful buildings!\" And Jesus answers, \"There will not be left here one stone upon another that will not be thrown down\" (Mark 13:1-2). The widow's offering sits between a warning about devoured widows and a prophecy of the building's destruction. Mark built that frame. Most of us preach the middle and skip both edges.\n\n## The Debate: Commendation or Lament?\n\nThe traditional reading takes Jesus's words as commendation. The widow is the pattern of true giving, measured by cost rather than sum. The rich gave \"out of their abundance\" — gifts that changed nothing about their lives. She gave everything. On this reading the text stands in a long biblical line — the widow of Zarephath giving her last flour to Elijah (1 Kings 17), the deep poverty of the Macedonian churches overflowing in generosity (2 Corinthians 8:1-3) — and its sermon writes itself: God's ledger runs on proportion.\n\nThere is a second reading, argued most fully by Ched Myers in *Binding the Strong Man* (1988) and taken up by other interpreters since: the scene is a lament. Jesus has just condemned the scribes who devour widows' houses; now he watches a widow being, in effect, devoured — giving \"all she had to live on\" to an institution that will commend her, hold her up as an example, and leave the conditions of her poverty untouched. On this reading Jesus is not celebrating the gift. He is grieving what the system does with such gifts, and the prophecy of the temple's destruction that follows is the verdict.\n\nThe honest sermon does not pretend this debate away. Notice, first, what the text does not say: Jesus never tells the disciples to imitate her, and he attaches no explicit word of praise to the act — the commendation reading supplies the tone. Notice, second, what the lament reading cannot erase: Jesus's sentence really is a statement of her gift's greatness against the gifts of the rich, and proportion really is its logic. Then notice what the two readings share, because that is where the preaching should land. On either reading, the ledger is proportion rather than sum. And on either reading, the institution is under judgment. The readings differ about the widow. They agree about the treasury.\n\n## The Tradition the Scene Stands In\n\nThe prophets had already said what Mark's frame implies: worship and justice are not separable line items.\n\n\"Take away from me the noise of your songs; to the melody of your harps I will not listen. But let justice roll down like waters, and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream\" (Amos 5:23-24). The quality of the music is irrelevant if the singers are crushing the poor.\n\nMicah aims directly at the building program — rulers \"who build Zion with blood and Jerusalem with iniquity,\" while the leaders \"lean on the LORD and say, 'Is not the LORD in the midst of us? No disaster shall come upon us'\" (Micah 3:10-11). Construction financed by injustice, insured — so they assumed — by God's presence in the building. Micah says the assumption is false.\n\nIsaiah opens his book with God refusing Israel's liturgy: \"When you spread out your hands, I will hide my eyes from you; even though you make many prayers, I will not listen; your hands are full of blood\" (Isaiah 1:15). The hands spread in prayer are the hands that did the exploiting. God looks past the posture to the ledger.\n\nJesus, seated opposite the treasury, is squarely in this tradition — watching the gap between what the institution collects and whom it protects.\n\n## Preaching It\n\nPreach the proportion. The text really does teach that God measures gifts by cost, not by amount, and a congregation formed on that principle will never again confuse a large donation with a great one.\n\nThen preach the frame, and let it turn toward us — because the modern preacher does not stand where the widow stands. We stand next to the treasury. I have preached the widow's mite during a building campaign and felt the text's approval resting on the budget. It was not resting there. The text was aimed at the treasury, and I was standing beside it. The sermon that only asks \"am I giving like the widow?\" has dodged the question the frame insists on: is my institution worthy of what the widows are giving it?\n\nAnd end the way Mark ends — without resolution. Jesus does not fix the widow's poverty, reform the treasury, or tell the disciples what should have been done. He names what happened, precisely, and then walks out of the temple for the last time.\n\nThe widow gave everything she had. The institution received it and went on being what it was.\n\nThe story ends there. The weight has not lifted.",
