    "id": 2000343,
    "title": "Formed by Screens: Technology and the Soul",
    "slug": "formed-by-screens-technology-and-the-soul",
    "body": "My youngest can find a video faster than I can find my keys. He is not corrupt. He is a boy with a boy's open face, and he loves his mother and asks me hard questions about God at bedtime. But I have watched something happen to his attention over a season, a fraying, a difficulty in staying with one slow thing, and I cannot honestly blame him, because I have watched the same fraying happen to me. I sit down to read Scripture and my hand reaches for the phone before my mind has settled, and the reaching is so automatic that it has stopped feeling like a choice. Something is forming us both, and it is not the Word.\n\nThe question is not whether technology is good or evil. That question is too small and too old, and it always ends in a shrug about how anything can be used well or badly. The real question is sharper. What kind of person does a given technology make of you simply by being used as it was designed to be used. Not abused. Used. Because every tool carries inside it a hidden curriculum, a way of forming the one who holds it, and the screen has a curriculum, and we have all enrolled, and most of us never read the syllabus.\n\n## The Hidden Curriculum of the Tool\n\nGive the strongest case for the screen, and give it honestly. These devices are astonishing gifts. I can carry the whole of Scripture, the Fathers, the Reformers, and a thousand sermons in one hand. A grandmother sees her grandchild's face across an ocean. A man in a remote village hears the gospel he could never have reached in print. The pandemic years would have been unbearable without the screen that kept the lonely connected to the living. To call all of this captivity would be ungrateful and untrue, the grumbling of a man who wishes for a past that was never as holy as he remembers. Technology is a good gift of a creative God to creative creatures. I use it to write the words you are reading. I will not pretend otherwise.\n\n> It abolishes the capacity for silence, and silence is where the soul learns to hear.\n\nBut here is what the grateful case keeps stepping around. A tool is not only what it does for you. It is what it does to you while you use it, and these two are often opposites. Charles Taylor described the modern self as buffered, sealed off from a world that once pressed in on it from every side, and the screen is the final architecture of that buffering, the wall made portable and pleasant (Taylor 2007). It promises to connect us and it insulates us. It puts a pane of glass between me and the man across the table, between me and the slow boredom out of which prayer is born, between me and my own unmanaged thoughts, which are exactly the thoughts in which God has always done his quietest work. The screen does not merely occupy the silence. It abolishes the capacity for silence, and silence is where the soul learns to hear.\n\n## The God Who Speaks in a Whisper\n\nCarry one text, and let it be strange enough to wake us. After the wind and the earthquake and the fire, none of which held the Lord, there came a low whisper. And when Elijah heard it, he wrapped his face in his cloak and went out (1 Kings 19:12 ESV). God was not in the spectacle. He was in the whisper, the sound that requires a quiet so deep that a man would have to have built his whole life around the practice of listening to hear it at all. Now ask the honest question. Could the version of me that checks the phone in the silence of his own prayers hear a whisper. The screen is a technology of the wind and the earthquake and the fire, endless spectacle, endless noise, and a God who speaks in a whisper is a God we have engineered ourselves to miss.\n\n## Technique Made Intimate\n\nTrace the history, because formation by tools is not new, only newly total. Jacques Ellul argued in the middle of the last century that the deepest danger of modern life is not any single machine but technique itself, the relentless drive toward efficiency that subordinates every human end to the question of what works, until the person is reshaped to serve the apparatus rather than the apparatus the person (Ellul 1964). The screen is technique made intimate. Neil Postman, building on this, warned that the move from the printed word to the image does not just change our entertainment, it changes the structure of our thought, training us for fragments and against arguments, for stimulation and against contemplation, until a whole civilization loses the patience that sustained things like reading a long book or sitting with a hard truth or praying without a phone in reach (Postman 1985). They saw the river's direction before most of us felt the current. We are now far downstream.\n\n## Formed by Ten Thousand Small Acts\n\nHere is the reversal, and it is the one that matters most. We believe we are using these devices to save time, and we are, and then we have no idea what the saved time was for, so we give it back to the same devices, and we are formed not by the content we consume but by the ten thousand small acts of consuming, the reach, the scroll, the flick, the refresh, repeated until they are no longer things we do but the shape of who we are. The danger was never that the screen would make us believe something false. The danger is that it would make us into a certain kind of soul, restless, distractible, incapable of the long slow attention that love and prayer and presence require, and that it would do this so gently we would mistake the new self for the only self we ever had.\n\n> I have been bodily present with my sons and mentally somewhere in a feed, and they know the difference even when I pretend they do not, because children read presence the way the rest of us read words.\n\nLaw before grace, and the law has my name on it. I have prayed with one ear on the notification. I have been bodily present with my sons and mentally somewhere in a feed, and they know the difference even when I pretend they do not, because children read presence the way the rest of us read words. I have told my congregation that God speaks in the whisper and then lived a life arranged so that no whisper could ever reach me. I am not the doctor here. I am the patient, and the diagnosis is the same one I would write for the man in my office who cannot sit still long enough to be loved by God.\n\n## Repentance With a Body\n\nThe grace is that we are formed creatures, which means we can be re formed, and the church has known the medicine for two thousand years. The old disciplines of silence and solitude and fasting are not nostalgia. They are deliberate counter formation, structured acts of refusing the wind and the earthquake and the fire so that the whisper has a chance to land. To put the phone in another room and sit in the unbearable quiet until it stops being unbearable is not a productivity hack. It is repentance with a body, the slow undoing of a formation we did not choose, the clearing of enough silence that a God who has been whispering the whole time can finally be heard saying what he said to Elijah, which was not condemnation but a question, gentle and terrible. What are you doing here.\n\n> The screen did not steal our time. It quietly stole the kind of soul that has time for God.\n\nThe screen did not steal our time. It quietly stole the kind of soul that has time for God.\n\nNot a tool we are using. A liturgy that is using us. We can still walk out into the silence, wrap our faces, and listen.\n\n### Works Cited\n\nTaylor, Charles. A Secular Age. Cambridge, MA: Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, 2007.\n\nEllul, Jacques. The Technological Society. Translated by John Wilkinson. New York: Vintage Books, 1964.\n\nPostman, Neil. Amusing Ourselves to Death: Public Discourse in the Age of Show Business. New York: Viking, 1985.\n\nThe Holy Bible. English Standard Version. Wheaton: Crossway, 2001.",
    "excerpt": "The screen does not only fill our time, it slowly remakes the kind of soul we have time for.",
