    "id": 2000348,
    "title": "Grace Is Not a Feeling",
    "slug": "grace-is-not-a-feeling",
    "body": "We have made grace into a mood. A soft light, a warm acceptance, a general sense that everything is fine and no one should feel bad about anything. And we have called that the heart of Christianity, when it is closer to its anesthetic.\n\n> It has a price, and the price is never paid by the one who receives it.\n\nGrace, in the New Testament, is not a feeling and not a temperament. It is charis, a gift, and specifically a gift handed to people who have not earned it and cannot repay it and may not even want it. It has a price, and the price is never paid by the one who receives it. That is the whole scandal of the word, and we have sanded the scandal down until grace means little more than being easygoing about things.\n\n## The Father Who Runs\n\nThe clearest picture Jesus ever drew of it is not gentle. A younger son demands his inheritance early, which in that world was a way of telling his father he wished him dead, and he takes the money and burns it on a far country, and he comes home only because he is starving and out of options. And the father, who has been watching the road, does the thing no patriarch with any standing would ever do. He runs. An old man gathers up his robe and runs down the road in front of the whole village to reach a son who shamed him, and he cuts off the boy's rehearsed apology before he can finish it, and he calls for the robe and the ring and the fattened calf for a son who deserves, at the very best, a servant's cot in the barn (Luke 15:11-24). That is grace. Notice that it costs the father his dignity, his wealth, and his right to be angry, and notice that the son does nothing to earn it except come home with empty hands. Robert Farrar Capon read the parables of grace as deliberately scandalous on exactly this point, insisting that grace operates with no regard for the recipient's merit and that any accounting of deserving has already missed what the story is doing (Capon 1985).\n\n## The Soft View at Its Strongest\n\nTake the soft view at its strongest, because it is guarding something true. The view that grace means God receives you exactly as you are, with no conditions, protects the gospel from the elder brother, the man who stayed home and kept every rule and despised his father's mercy. A great many people were raised on shame and need to hear, with no asterisk, that the love came first and uninvited, before they ever cleaned themselves up. That is not a distortion. It is in the parable. The father runs before the son says a word.\n\nBut the soft view quietly forgets the cost, and a grace that costs nothing changes nothing. Dietrich Bonhoeffer gave the failure its permanent name. Cheap grace is the deadly enemy of the church. It is the grace we bestow on ourselves, the preaching of forgiveness without requiring repentance, communion without confession, grace without discipleship, grace without the cross (Bonhoeffer 1937). The father's welcome was not the absence of a reckoning. It ran straight through the death of his own honor in front of his neighbors. Costly grace, Bonhoeffer said, is the treasure hidden in the field, the pearl worth selling everything to buy, the call that sends a man from his nets.\n\n## The Grace That Costs Everything\n\nSteel-man the opposite fear too, because the legalist is not imagining things. He worries that free grace becomes a license, that if forgiveness is unearned people will sin all the more freely. Paul raises the very objection himself. Are we to continue in sin that grace may abound. His answer is not a lecture but an astonishment. By no means. How can we who died to sin still live in it (Romans 6:1-2 ESV). The worry mistakes what grace is. Grace is not permission. It is power. It never says your sin did not matter. It says your sin has been paid for at a price that, the moment you actually see it, makes you never want to return to it.\n\n> Costly grace produces sons.\n\nHere is the reversal the soft view never sees coming. The grace that asks nothing of you is not kinder than the grace that asks everything. It is crueler, because it leaves you precisely where it found you. A love that requires no change is a love that has quietly given up on you, that has decided you are not worth the trouble of being made new. Timothy Keller, reading this parable, points out that there are two lost sons in it, and the dutiful elder brother seething outside the feast is the one the story leaves standing in the dark, because he wanted the father's things and not the father, and his rule-keeping was only a more respectable way of running from him (Keller 2008). Cheap grace produces elder brothers. Costly grace produces sons.\n\n## Grace Is Power, Not Permission\n\nI preach grace for a living, and I am most tempted to cheapen it precisely because it is the loveliest word I know. It is easy to stand up and tell people God loves them as they are and send them home unchanged and feeling fine, and to call that a gospel of grace, when it is closer to a sedative. The grace that actually saved me was never the one that told me I was fine. It was the one that told me the truth about what I had done and ran down the road to take hold of me in the middle of the telling.\n\n## Why Cheap Grace Is the Crueler Love\n\nGrace is not a feeling. It is a father on a road in front of a village, his robe in his hands and his dignity on the ground, reaching a son who deserved none of it. It cost him everything and asked the son for nothing but the long walk home. That is not soft. It is the hardest and most total thing in the world, a love you cannot earn and cannot repay and cannot survive unchanged. Anything gentler than that is not grace. It is the word grace, with the cross removed.\n\n> Grace is not a feeling.\n\n### Works Cited\n\nBonhoeffer, Dietrich. 1937. The Cost of Discipleship. Translated by R. H. Fuller. New York: Macmillan.\n\nCapon, Robert Farrar. 1985. The Parables of Grace. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans.\n\nKeller, Timothy. 2008. The Prodigal God. New York: Dutton.",
    "excerpt": "We turned grace into a warm mood that asks nothing and changes no one. Real grace costs the giver everything, and that is why it is not soft.",
