    "id": 2000387,
    "title": "The Image of God and the Lie of Race",
    "slug": "the-image-of-god-and-the-lie-of-race",
    "body": "Ask a child where race comes from and she will tell you it has always been here, that people simply are these colors and these kinds, the way the sky is blue. Ask a historian and you get a different answer. Race as we mean it, the sorting of humanity into a ladder of worth by the color of skin, is younger than the printing press. It was made. And the making has a date, a motive, and a set of hands, and some of those hands held a Bible.\n\n## The Floor Beneath Every Human\n\nThe text I want to carry is the oldest claim Scripture makes about a human being. God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him, male and female he created them (Genesis 1:27 ESV). The imago dei. Before nation, before tribe, before anything we call race, there is this. Every human being bears the image of God, and that image is not distributed by degree. It is not more present in one people and less in another. It is the floor under all of us, the thing that makes a person a person, and Scripture lays it down in the first chapter before sin has even entered the story.\n\nHere is the strong objection, and I want it stated at full strength. Many thoughtful people say the church should stop talking about race entirely, that the very category is the problem, that to keep naming Black and white is to keep alive a fiction we should let die. Color blindness, they argue, is the Christian position. We are all one in Christ, there is neither Jew nor Greek, so why keep sorting the body by a distinction the gospel abolished. There is something genuinely right in this, and I will not pretend otherwise. The category of race is a lie. It has no basis in biology. The differences we have built a ladder upon are surface deep, and the gospel really does make one new humanity out of the many.\n\n## Why Color Blindness Fails\n\nBut here is where the color-blind answer fails, and it fails for a reason worth understanding. You cannot heal a wound by refusing to name the weapon. Race is a lie, yes. But it is a lie that built a world, that wrote laws, that drew maps, that decided who got a loan and who got a noose, and you do not undo a four-hundred-year-old structure by closing your eyes to it. Telling a man whose grandfather was redlined out of a neighborhood that you do not see color does not return the house. It only blinds you to why he does not have one. The lie of race must be named precisely because it is a lie that did real damage, and the damage did not vanish when we stopped saying the quiet part out loud.\n\n## The Manufacture of Race\n\n> You cannot heal a wound by refusing to name the weapon.\n\nTrace the invention. Before the European slave trade, people knew themselves by tribe, by city, by faith, by tongue. There were enslaved people of every shade, and slavery, evil as it was, was not yet organized around color. Then came the seventeenth century and a problem the planters needed to solve. African labor was being held for life while European labor was held for years, and that difference needed a justification that could survive a Christian conscience. So the category was built. Willie James Jennings shows how European Christians, in the age of conquest, began to read the peoples of the earth through a new lens, with whiteness placed at the top as the measure of the human and Blackness at the bottom, and how theology lent its weight to the sorting (Jennings 2010). Race was not discovered. It was theologically manufactured to make a sin look like an order of creation.\n\n> Race was not discovered. It was theologically manufactured to make a sin look like an order of creation.\n\nThe church did not merely permit this. It supplied the verses. The so-called curse of Ham, drawn from a misreading of Genesis 9, was preached for centuries as the divine charter for African slavery, the claim that a whole people had been cursed to servitude by the word of God. It was a lie laid on top of the lie of race, Scripture twisted to baptize an economy. Esau McCaulley, reading the Bible from within the Black church tradition, shows how the same texts the slaveholder weaponized were read by the enslaved as a charter of dignity and deliverance, how Reading While Black means refusing both the oppressor's distortion and the despair it was meant to produce (McCaulley 2020). The book that was used to forge the chain was the same book that named the prisoner an image-bearer. Only one of those readings was true.\n\n## The Sin Is the Ladder\n\nHere is the reversal, and it is the heart of the matter. We tend to think the problem of race is a problem of difference, that if we could only see past our differences we would be at peace. But Genesis says the opposite. The problem is not that we are different. God made the difference and called the whole creation very good. The problem is that we took a difference God made and built a hierarchy God never authorized. The sin is not color. The sin is the ladder. And the cure for a ladder is not to pretend everyone is on the same rung by ignoring the ranks. The cure is to tear the ladder down and confess we built it.\n\n## The Image as Law and Grace\n\n> The sin is not color. The sin is the ladder.\n\nThe imago dei does real work here, and it does it as law before it does it as grace. As law, it indicts. If every human bears the image of God, then every act of the slave trade, every lynching, every redlined map, every sermon that ranked the races was an assault on the image of God, a defacing of his likeness in the person of his neighbor. There is no neutral ground. To diminish a human being by color is to spit on the face of God reflected in that human being, and the church that taught the diminishing taught blasphemy with a Bible open. I am in that church's lineage. So is every white believer in America. The indictment includes us.\n\nAnd as grace, the imago dei restores. The image cannot be erased by any lie. Four hundred years of teaching that Black bodies were worth less did not make them worth less, because worth was never ours to assign. It was set by God in the first chapter and sealed by God in the incarnation, when the Word took on flesh and dignified the human body forever. Augustine wrote that the image of God in us, defaced by sin, is being renewed in those who turn to Christ, restored to the likeness it was made for (Augustine, The Trinity 14). The lie of race tried to deface the image. The gospel renews it. And a church renewed in the image of God cannot keep the ladder. The renewal and the ladder cannot stand in the same body.\n\nSo we do not get color blindness, and we do not get the ladder either. We get something harder than both. We get the call to name the lie for what it is, to confess the hands that made it, and to look at every human face, including the faces our fathers ranked, and see what was always there.\n\nThe image of God. Defaced for four centuries. Never once erased.\n\n### Works Cited\n\nAugustine. The Trinity. Translated by Edmund Hill. Brooklyn: New City Press, 1991.\n\nJennings, Willie James. The Christian Imagination: Theology and the Origins of Race. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2010.\n\nMcCaulley, Esau. Reading While Black: African American Biblical Interpretation as an Exercise in Hope. Downers Grove: InterVarsity Press, 2020.",
    "excerpt": "Race is a recent invention dressed up as an ancient fact, and the church helped sew the costume.",
    "pillar": "prophetic-justice",
    "readTime": "10 min read",
