    "id": 2000388,
    "title": "Lament: The Prayer the Church Forgot",
    "slug": "lament-the-prayer-the-church-forgot",
    "body": "Open the hymnal of most white churches and try to find a song that does not resolve. Try to find one that ends in the question and not the answer, that leaves the grief standing, that does not hurry to the bridge where everything is fine. You will be looking a long time. We have a thousand songs of triumph and almost none of sorrow, and that absence is not a matter of taste. It is a theology, and it has cost us the ability to speak honestly about race.\n\n## The Prayers That Do Not Resolve\n\n> Lament is not the opposite of faith. It is faith refusing to lie about the state of the world.\n\nA full third of the Psalms are laments, prayers of complaint, grief, and protest addressed to God. They are not polite. How long, O Lord. Will you forget me forever (Psalm 13:1 ESV). My God, my God, why have you forsaken me (Psalm 22:1 ESV). These are not the prayers of people whose faith is failing. They are the prayers of the most faithful people in the Bible, and Jesus himself died with one of them on his lips. Lament is not the opposite of faith. It is faith refusing to lie about the state of the world.\n\nLet me carry one psalm as the spine. Psalm 13 moves in three movements, and the church has learned to live only in the last. It opens in complaint, four times the cry how long, the prayer of a man who feels forgotten by the God he still addresses. Then it turns to petition, consider and answer me, light up my eyes. And only at the end does it arrive at trust, I will sing to the Lord because he has dealt bountifully with me (Psalm 13:6 ESV). Three movements. Complaint, petition, trust. We skip the first two and sing only the third, and a third of a prayer is not a prayer. It is a performance of arrival we have not actually made.\n\n## Grief as the Road to Hope\n\nNow the strong objection, because there is one. Some will say that lament risks becoming a wallow, that endless grief over race keeps wounds open that grace means to close, that the gospel calls us to rejoice and to forgive and to look forward, not to camp in lamentation as if the resurrection had not happened. They say a faith fixated on sorrow has forgotten the empty tomb. And there is truth here worth holding. The Christian does not grieve as one without hope. Lament that never moves toward trust curdles into despair, and despair is not a Christian word.\n\nBut notice what the objection assumes. It assumes that the choice is between grief and hope, when the Psalms refuse that choice entirely. Psalm 13 does not arrive at trust by skipping the complaint. It arrives at trust through the complaint, by way of it, having spoken the grief fully to God first. The hope at the end of the psalm is earned, weighted, real, precisely because the sorrow at the beginning was not censored. A church that cannot lament cannot truly hope either, because its hope has never passed through honesty. It has only passed around it. And a hope that goes around the wound is a denial wearing the mask of faith.\n\n## The Liturgical Failure of the White Church\n\nHere is where this touches race directly. The reason the white church struggles to speak about race is, in part, a liturgical failure. We have no language for it. We know how to celebrate and we know how to instruct, but we do not know how to grieve, because we deleted grief from our worship generations ago. So when the history of slavery and lynching and segregation is set before us, we have no vessel to receive it. We reach for explanation, for defense, for the quick turn to reconciliation, because we never learned the prayer that simply sits in the wreckage and says to God, how long. The Black church kept that prayer. The spirituals were lament, the slave singing nobody knows the trouble I have seen straight into the face of God. We who lost lament lost the very language the moment required.\n\n## The Lynchings We Never Mourned\n\n> We never even learned to cry about it.\n\nThe history is specific and the silence around it is the proof. From 1877 to 1950 the historians at the Equal Justice Initiative have documented more than four thousand racial terror lynchings in the American South, public killings often attended by churchgoing crowds, sometimes on a Sunday, sometimes with the body displayed near a house of worship. The white church of that era did not lament these murders. It largely watched, or looked away, or in some cases supplied the crowd. There is no widely sung hymn of grief for them. The absence of that lament, eighty years on, is itself a sin we have not confessed. We never even learned to cry about it.\n\nWalter Brueggemann argues that the loss of lament is the loss of a whole dimension of faith, that a community which can only praise has surrendered its capacity to hold God accountable and to tell the truth about its own pain (Brueggemann 1986). Without lament, he says, the covenant becomes one-sided, a relationship in which the powerful are never questioned and the suffering are never heard. A church that cannot lament cannot do justice, because justice begins with the truthful naming of what is wrong, and that naming, addressed to God, is exactly what lament is. We traded the prayer for a smile, and we lost the truth in the trade.\n\n## Lament as Intimacy\n\nHere is the reversal. We have treated lament as a sign of weak faith, a stage to grow out of, a sorrow that mature believers transcend. But the Bible treats lament as a sign of intimacy. You do not complain to a stranger. You complain to a father, to the one you trust enough to be honest with, to the one you believe is listening and able. The freedom to cry how long, O Lord is the freedom of a child who knows the father is there. To refuse lament is not strength. It is distance. It is a politeness that has stopped believing God can take the truth.\n\n> A church too proud to grieve is a church that thinks itself above its own Lord.\n\nThe cross does the deepest work here, and it does it before it comforts. On the cross Jesus prayed Psalm 22, my God, my God, why have you forsaken me, and in praying it he sanctified lament forever. God himself, in the flesh, lamented. The sinless one took the prayer of grief onto his own lips and made it holy. So there is no spiritual height above lament, because the highest one stooped into it. The law here is plain. A church too proud to grieve is a church that thinks itself above its own Lord.\n\nAnd the grace is this. Because Christ lamented and was heard, our laments are heard too. The how long is not a cry into an empty sky. It rises to a God who has himself cried it, who knows the weight of the forsaken from the inside, who will not despise the broken prayer because he prayed it first. We are free to grieve all the way down, because the bottom is not despair. The bottom is a man on a cross who got there before us.\n\nWe forgot how to lament, and forgetting it, we lost the only honest language we had for what race has done. The first repentance may be smaller and harder than we think. Not a program. A prayer. How long, O Lord. We have not even begun to pray it.\n\n### Works Cited\n\nBrueggemann, Walter. The Costly Loss of Lament. Journal for the Study of the Old Testament 36 (1986): 57-71.\n\nEqual Justice Initiative. Lynching in America: Confronting the Legacy of Racial Terror. Montgomery: Equal Justice Initiative, 2017.\n\nThurman, Howard. Jesus and the Disinherited. Boston: Beacon Press, 1949.",
    "excerpt": "We taught ourselves to skip past the prayers of grief, and in skipping them we lost the only language honest enough to carry what race has done.",
    "pillar": "prophetic-justice",
    "readTime": "9 min read",
