    "id": 2000411,
    "title": "What the Bible Says About the Poor and the Stranger",
    "slug": "what-the-bible-says-about-the-poor-and-the-stranger",
    "body": "A man sleeps under the overpass two blocks from our church. I have driven past him on my way to preach about him. That is not a confession I enjoy making, but it is the truest sentence I can write to begin, because the question of what the Bible says about the poor is never first a question of exegesis. It is a question of who we have trained ourselves not to see. The text was never the problem. We were.\n\n## The Question Beneath the Exegesis\n\nThe real question is not whether Scripture cares about the poor. Even the most casual reader knows it does. The real question is why a book so relentless on this subject has produced a church so comfortable with poverty. Hundreds of passages across the law and the prophets and the Gospels touch wealth and want, the orphan and the widow, the laborer and the gleaner. We have read them and built sanctuaries with good parking. Something in us has learned to receive these texts as decoration rather than command.\n\n> The text was never the problem. We were.\n\nThe strongest objection deserves a fair hearing, because it is not stupid and it is not made only by the hard of heart. It runs like this. The biblical commands about the poor were given to a covenant nation, ancient Israel, a theocracy with specific agrarian laws for a specific people in a specific land. Gleaning laws assume fields. Jubilee assumes tribal allotment. The year of release assumes a shared bloodline. To lift these statutes out of Iron Age Canaan and drop them onto a modern pluralistic economy is, the argument goes, a category mistake. And the political reading of these texts has often been lazy, baptizing whatever policy the reader already preferred. That critique lands. The Bible is not a budget proposal, and people who quote Leviticus to win an election usually have not read Leviticus.\n\n## The Strongest Objection: Laws for a Covenant Nation\n\nBut here the strong objection breaks on a single word, and the word is ger. The sojourner. The stranger. The resident alien who belonged to no tribe, held no allotment, and stood outside the bloodline entirely. If the poverty laws were merely tribal housekeeping, the ger should have been excluded from them by definition. He is the one person the covenant could have ignored without contradiction. Instead he is named again and again, woven into the very statutes the objection says were for insiders only. 'When you reap the harvest of your land, you shall not reap your field right up to its edge, neither shall you gather the gleanings after your harvest. You shall leave them for the poor and for the sojourner' (Leviticus 23:22 ESV). The stranger gleans the same field as the widow. The law reaches past the boundary it supposedly defends.\n\n## The Stranger Who Breaks the Argument\n\nThis is the text I want to carry, because the ger exposes the engine underneath the whole code. Why include the outsider. The answer is given without argument, as a brute fact of Israel's identity. 'You shall love the sojourner, for you were sojourners in the land of Egypt' (Deuteronomy 10:19 ESV). The memory of slavery is the foundation of mercy. Israel is commanded to treat the vulnerable as it wished it had been treated when it was vulnerable, when it had no land, no rights, no name worth recording in Pharaoh's ledgers. The poverty laws are not tribal property management. They are the moral residue of a rescue. A redeemed people remembers the pit.\n\nThe Hebrew has a word for the whole class of the unprotected, the anawim. The bowed-down ones. Abraham Heschel, reading the prophets, saw that their fury was never abstract. 'The prophet's word is a scream in the night,' he wrote, and while the world lies at ease and asleep, the prophet feels the blast from heaven (Heschel 1962). Amos does not condemn Israel for theological error in the seminary sense. He condemns men who 'trample the head of the poor into the dust of the earth' (Amos 2:7 ESV), who sell the righteous for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals. Isaiah indicts a worship that runs perfectly while the orphan goes uncared for, fasts that God refuses to smell because the fast he chose was 'to share your bread with the hungry and bring the homeless poor into your house' (Isaiah 58:7 ESV). The prophets did not separate the altar from the street. We did. That separation is our invention, not theirs.\n\n## The Prophets Who Refused to Divide Altar From Street\n\n> The prophets did not separate the altar from the street. We did.\n\nTrace the historical root and the picture sharpens uncomfortably. The early church took these texts at face value in ways that scandalized Rome. By the fourth century the emperor Julian, who hated Christianity and tried to roll it back, complained in a letter around 362 that the 'impious Galileans support not only their own poor but ours as well.' The pagan world noticed Christians feeding strangers who were not theirs. Then came Constantine, then came land, then came endowment, and slowly the church that had been a refuge for the ger became a property owner with the ger outside its gates. The Reformers fought over grace and missed the poor. The American church, born in a land of seemingly endless property, baptized acquisition as blessing and read the poverty texts as metaphors for spiritual lack. We spiritualized hunger so we would not have to feed it. I have preached that sermon. I am inside this.\n\n## How the Church Learned to Spiritualize Hunger\n\nAnd here the reversal, because the gospel does not leave the comfortable reader where it found him. We imagine ourselves the gleaners, the generous ones with full fields choosing to leave a corner. Scripture will not let us keep that seat. The whole arc of redemption casts us not as the landowner but as the ger, the stranger with no claim, the bowed-down one who was 'alienated from the commonwealth of Israel and strangers to the covenants of promise, having no hope and without God in the world' (Ephesians 2:12 ESV). We are the sojourner. We did not earn our place at the table. It was given to us by a God who remembered our slavery when we had forgotten it ourselves.\n\nThis is where the cross does the argument's real work, and not as sentiment. The law demanded the gleaning, the open hand, the remembered pit, and we did not keep it. We reaped to the edges. We trampled heads into dust. The law's verdict on us is just, and it is severe, and we cannot answer it. So Christ became poor. 'For your sake he became poor, so that you by his poverty might become rich' (2 Corinthians 8:9 ESV). He took the position of the anawim absolutely, naked on a Roman beam, executed outside the city wall where the unclean and the foreign were thrown. The one with every claim took the place of the one with none. Mercy to the poor is not a moral upgrade we add to faith. It is the faith, worked outward, because we have received exactly the mercy the poverty laws describe. We give what we were given. To withhold it is to forget Egypt.\n\n## The Cross and the Poverty of Christ\n\nThe man under the overpass is still there tonight. I know his approximate location and not his name. The Bible says what it says about the poor and the stranger, with a clarity that does not require a scholar to decode, and the distance between that clarity and my life is not a gap in my theology. It is the gap. Heschel said the prophet's task was to make us feel the scream we had learned to sleep through. The text has done its work. The field still has corners.\n\n> We give what we were given. To withhold it is to forget Egypt.\n\nWe were sojourners in the land of Egypt. We are not allowed to forget it.\n\n### Works Cited\n\nHeschel, Abraham J. The Prophets. New York: Harper and Row, 1962.\n\nThe Holy Bible, English Standard Version. Wheaton: Crossway, 2001.",
    "excerpt": "Scripture returns to the poor and the stranger hundreds of times, across law and prophets and Gospels, and the church has learned to read past every one of them.",
